Make abd_model.tiles.tiles_from_dir work on Windows paths.

Upstream builds a regex by concatenating `os.path.join(root, "(?P<z>...)")`
and matching against a discovered path. On Windows, `os.path.join` inserts
`\\` separators, which the regex engine interprets as escape sequences,
producing `re.error: bad escape \i at position ...`. The regex also uses
`/` as a literal separator between the (z,x,y) groups, but the discovered
paths use `\\`.

Fix: `re.escape(root)` and match either `/` or `\\` as the separator
between z, x, y. Behaviour on POSIX is unchanged (forward slash still
matches).

diff --git a/abd_model/src/abd_model/tiles.py b/abd_model/src/abd_model/tiles.py
--- a/abd_model/src/abd_model/tiles.py
+++ b/abd_model/src/abd_model/tiles.py
@@ -70,7 +70,10 @@ def tiles_from_dir(root, cover=None, xyz=True, xyz_path=False):
         paths = glob.glob(os.path.join(root, "[0-9]*/[0-9]*/[0-9]*.*"))

         for path in paths:
-            tile_xyz = re.match(os.path.join(root, "(?P<z>[0-9]+)/(?P<x>[0-9]+)/(?P<y>[0-9]+).+"), path)
+            tile_xyz = re.match(
+                re.escape(os.path.join(root, "")) + r"(?P<z>[0-9]+)[\\/](?P<x>[0-9]+)[\\/](?P<y>[0-9]+).+",
+                path,
+            )
             if not tile_xyz:
                 continue
             tile = mercantile.Tile(int(tile_xyz["x"]), int(tile_xyz["y"]), int(tile_xyz["z"]))
